    <article class="article">
      <p class="eyebrow">Lexicon entry · planetary relation</p>
      <h1>Reception</h1>
      <p class="lede">A relationship in which one planet is received through a dignity belonging to another. Historical authors do not formulate the doctrine in exactly the same way, so their accounts remain separately attributable.</p>

      <div class="mode-switch" aria-label="Display mode">
        <button class="active" data-mode="reading">Reading</button>
        <button data-mode="sources">Editorial detail</button>
      </div>

      <div class="definition-panel" id="definition">
        <p class="micro-label">Canonical definition</p>
        <p>Reception is the relation created when a planet occupies a dignity belonging to another planet. Mutual reception occurs when the relationship is reciprocal. Historical authors differ over which dignities qualify, how strongly they operate, and what reception accomplishes in judgment.</p>
        <p class="sources-only meta">Editorial synthesis: the public definition is deliberately broader than any one witness and must remain traceable to the reviewed source accounts below.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="names">
        <h2>Names & related forms</h2>
        <div class="term-links">
          <span>Reception</span>
          <span>Mutual reception</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="sources">
        <h2>Source accounts</h2>

        <div class="source-block" id="biruni">
          <div class="source-head">
            <h3>al-Bīrūnī · <em>Book of Instruction</em></h3>
            <span class="source-label">§507 · pp. 330–331</span>
          </div>
          <p>al-Bīrūnī describes reception through a planet arriving in a dignity proper to another planet. If the second planet is likewise in a place proper to the first, the relationship becomes mutual reception. He adds that the condition is strengthened when the relevant places contain richer dignities and that hostile configurations can qualify the relationship.</p>
          <p class="source-cite"><span class="evidence">A</span>R. Ramsay Wright translation · direct source definition.</p>
          <div class="sources-only">
            <p class="meta">The database stores the interpretation separately from the source passage and links them through a citation record. Public wording is paraphrase rather than reproduced source text.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="source-block" id="lilly">
          <div class="source-head">
            <h3>William Lilly · <em>Christian Astrology</em></h3>
            <span class="source-label">Book I · Ch. XIX · p. 112</span>
          </div>
          <p>Lilly presents reception as a reciprocal relation between significators occupying one another’s dignities. Reception by house is treated as the strongest form in this discussion, while he also allows reception through triplicity, term, face and other essential dignity. In horary judgment he gives mutual reception substantial practical weight.</p>
          <p class="source-cite"><span class="evidence">A</span>1647 text, digital retype · direct source definition.</p>
          <div class="sources-only">
            <p class="meta">The final database-driven page will render the exact edition metadata, passage locator, evidence grade and any additional reviewed witnesses automatically.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="comparison">
        <h2>Historical comparison</h2>
        <p>The current witnesses already show why reception should not be compressed into a single modern rule. al-Bīrūnī emphasizes reception through another planet’s dignity, reciprocal reception and qualitative strengthening. Lilly frames the doctrine in a more explicitly practical hierarchy and applies it directly to horary perfection.</p>
        <p class="meta">The complete database contains later cross-source revision work, so this manually written showcase will ultimately be replaced by the same query-driven template as every other term.</p>
      </section>

